-- title:
--   Restrictedness of the formal inverse via a polynomial inverse mod p
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal O$ be a commutative ring, let $p$ be a natural number and let $d$ be a natural number. Let $\varphi,\psi\colon \mathrm{Fin}\,d \to \mathcal O[[X_1,\dots,X_d]]$ be two $d$-tuples of formal power series in $d$ variables over $\mathcal O$. Assume: each $\varphi_i$ has vanishing constant coefficient; the linear part of $\varphi$, namely the matrix whose $(i,j)$ entry is the coefficient of $\varphi_i$ at the monomial $X_j$, is the identity matrix; $\varphi$ is restricted in the sense that for every $N$ and every $i$, all but finitely many coefficients of $\varphi_i$ lie in the ideal generated by $p^N$; and for all $i,j$ and every exponent vector $m$ of total degree at least $2$, the product of the image of $m_j$ in $\mathcal O$ with the coefficient of $\varphi_i$ at $m$ lies in the ideal generated by $p$. Assume further that each $\psi_i$ has vanishing constant coefficient and that $\psi$ is a two-sided inverse of $\varphi$ under substitution: $\varphi \circ \psi = X$ and $\psi \circ \varphi = X$ componentwise. Finally, assume given a $d$-tuple $\chi$ of polynomials in $d$ variables over $\mathcal O$ with vanishing constant coefficients such that, for each $i$ and each exponent vector $m$, the $m$-th coefficient of the substitution of $\chi$ into $\varphi_i$ differs from that of $X_i$ by an element of the ideal generated by $p$. Then $\psi$ is restricted as well: for every $N$ and every $i$, all but finitely many coefficients of $\psi_i$ lie in the ideal generated by $p^N$.
--
--   This is a Hensel-type statement for restricted (Tate-algebra-like) power series in several variables: an invertible substitution whose coefficients tend to zero $p$-adically, whose linear part is the identity and whose Jacobian is congruent to the identity modulo $p$, has a restricted inverse as soon as some polynomial tuple inverts it modulo $p$. No primality of $p$, completeness of $\mathcal O$ or convergence hypothesis enters. It is used in the analysis of rescaled exponentials of multivariable formal groups over local rings with prescribed Cartier dual behaviour.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_MvPowerSeries_eventually_coeff_mem_span_pow_of_subst_eq_X_of_exists_polynomial_inverse_mod.lean

